        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "check-newtons-third-law-2"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$a = \\frac{20}{10} = 2 \\text{ m/s}^2$$ (away from astronaut)\n\nAstronaut acceleration:\n$$a = \\frac{20}{70} = 0.29 \\text{ m/s}^2$$ (away from toolbox)\n\nBoth move apart, but the toolbox accelerates faster.\n\n## Applications\n\n### Walking\n\n1. Your foot pushes backward on the ground\n2. The ground pushes forward on your foot (Third Law)\n3. This forward force accelerates you\n\n```\n       Person\n        /|\\\n       / | \\\n      /  |  \\\n     /   |   \\\n    /    |    \\\n   ------+------\n   Foot pushes     Ground pushes\n   ground BACKWARD foot FORWARD\n   <--- F1         F2 --->\n\n   F1 = F2 (Third Law pair)\n   The forward force F2 accelerates the person\n```\n\n### Swimming\n\n1. Hands push water backward\n2. Water pushes hands (and swimmer) forward\n\n### Rocket Propulsion\n\n1. Rocket pushes exhaust gases downward\n2. Exhaust gases push rocket upward\n\nThis works even in the vacuum of space because the reaction force acts on the rocket, not on air.\n\n### Recoil\n\nWhen a gun fires:\n1. Gun pushes bullet forward\n2. Bullet pushes gun backward (recoil)\n\nBoth forces have equal magnitude, but the gun has much more mass, so it accelerates less.\n\n## Common Misconceptions\n\n1. **\"Action and reaction cancel out\"** — They act on DIFFERENT objects, so they cannot cancel.\n\n2. **\"The reaction happens after the action\"** — Both forces occur simultaneously. \"Action\" and \"reaction\" are just names; neither comes first.\n\n3. **\"Weight and normal reaction are a Third Law pair\"** — No! They act on the same object. Weight pairs with the object pulling Earth; normal reaction pairs with the object pushing the surface.\n\n4. **\"Heavier objects exert more force\"** — The forces are always equal. The heavier object accelerates less because of its greater mass.\n\n5. **\"Rockets push against the air\"** — Rockets work by pushing exhaust gases. They work in space where there's no air.\n\n6. **\"If forces are equal, nothing moves\"** — Objects move based on the NET force ON THEM. Third Law pairs act on different objects.\n\n## Practice Problems\n\n1. State the force the cart exerts on the horse when a horse pulls a cart forward with a force of 1200 N.\n\n2. Calculate the force the wall exerts on you if you push it with 80 N. Describe what happens to you if the floor is frictionless.\n\n3. A 60 kg person and a 90 kg person on ice push against each other with equal forces of 120 N. Calculate the acceleration of each person.\n\n4. State the Newton's Third Law pair for each force:\n   a) A bat hits a ball with 500 N\n   b) Earth's gravity pulls the Moon\n   c) Air resistance pushes backward on a car\n\n5. A book of weight 15 N rests on a table. The table exerts an upward force of 15 N on the book. Explain why the weight and the normal reaction are NOT a Third Law pair. State the actual Third Law pairs for each force.\n\n6. A 2000 kg car pushes backward on the road with 6000 N. The road pushes forward on the car with 6000 N. If friction and air resistance total 2000 N, calculate the car's acceleration.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Newton's Third Law: Every action has an equal and opposite reaction\n- Third Law pairs are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, same type, act on different objects\n- Forces don't cancel because they act on different objects\n- Applications: walking, swimming, rockets, recoil\n- Weight and normal reaction are NOT a Third Law pair\n- Both forces in a pair act simultaneously\n- Equal forces on different masses cause different accelerations\n\n## Answer Key\n\n1. **1200 N backward (on the horse)** — By Newton's Third Law, the cart pulls back on the horse with an equal and opposite force of 1200 N (explanation with physics reasoning)\n\n2. **80 N** — The wall pushes back on you with 80 N (Third Law). On a frictionless floor, this unbalanced force would accelerate you backward, away from the wall (explanation with physics reasoning)\n\n3. **60 kg person: 2 m/s²; 90 kg person: 1.33 m/s²** — $a_{60} = 120/60 = 2$ m/s²; $a_{90} = 120/90 = 1.33$ m/s²; they accelerate in opposite directions (calculation)\n\n4. **a)** The ball exerts a 500 N force on the bat (in the opposite direction) (explanation with physics reasoning)\n   **b)** The Moon's gravity pulls the Earth (with equal magnitude, toward the Moon) (explanation with physics reasoning)\n   **c)** The car pushes the air forward (with equal magnitude) (explanation with physics reasoning)\n\n5. Weight and normal reaction are NOT a Third Law pair because: they act on the same object (the book), and they are different types of force (gravitational vs contact). The actual pairs are: (i) weight of book (Earth pulls book down) pairs with the book pulling Earth up; (ii) normal reaction (table pushes book up) pairs with the book pushing the table down (explanation with physics reasoning)\n\n6. **2 m/s²** — The forward force from the road on the car is 6000 N. Net force $= 6000 - 2000 = 4000$ N forward. $a = F/m = 4000/2000 = 2$ m/s² (calculation)$$"
          }
        ]
